WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.599
A tool returns nothing. That single result can mean two completely different things and an agent

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:11.119
has no way to tell the difference. This is a silent bug and it's one of the hardest failures

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:16.319
to catch in production. For the next few minutes we'll look at how it happens and what it can cost

4
00:00:16.319 --> 00:00:22.079
you. Let's get into it. Picture a search that comes back with nothing. The agent can't see

5
00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:27.680
whether the data source was checked and came up empty or whether the tool never reached it at all.

6
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.880
An access failure is when a tool call doesn't successfully reach the underlying data source.

7
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.360
Connection refused. Credentials rejected. Request timed out. The tool never got to search. An empty

8
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.799
result is the opposite. The tool reached the data source just fine and confirmed there is

9
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:50.720
nothing matching your query. That is a legitimate answer. The silent false negative is what happens

10
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.799
when an agent reports nothing exists because a tool failed silently rather than because the

11
00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:01.840
data is genuinely absent. Keep those distinct in your mental model and the rest of this lesson

12
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:08.000
follows naturally. Let me give you a concrete picture of how this bug happens. Imagine a search

13
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.000
tool that queries a vector index for relevant documents. At some point during an agent session

14
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.480
the index service goes offline. The tool still runs. It sends a query but instead of getting

15
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.760
search results it gets a connection error internally and it returns an empty list to the

16
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.760
agent with no error flag attached. The agent reads that empty list. It has been instructed

17
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.839
to trust tool results so it concludes that no documents exist and moves forward confidently

18
00:01:37.839 --> 00:01:44.400
with that conclusion. The silent bug is now alive in the session. The failure and a successful empty

19
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.639
search look identical from the agent's perspective. That is the core problem we're solving in this

20
00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:57.519
lesson. The distinction between these two outcomes is the most important idea in this lesson. An

21
00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:02.480
access failure means the tool couldn't reach the data source. The empty response reflects a

22
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:09.199
connection problem not a content gap. Retrying the call is appropriate and the agent shouldn't draw

23
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:15.520
any conclusions about what exists or doesn't exist in the data store. An empty result means the tool

24
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.039
successfully reached the data source and confirmed there is nothing matching the query. That is a

25
00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:27.839
valid answer. Retrying is wasteful. The absence is meaningful information. The problem is that

26
00:02:27.839 --> 00:02:33.600
without explicit signaling both outcomes look like an empty list to the agent. It can't tell

27
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:40.639
them apart on its own. You have to build that distinction into the tool response. Why do agents

28
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.160
default to treating an empty result as nothing exists? Because that is what the prompt and the

29
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.240
model's pattern matching push them toward. An empty list is a data signal. The model is trained to

30
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.240
treat data signals as ground truth. When a tool returns an empty list the implicit message is

31
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.880
I searched and found nothing. There is no error flag, no exception, no branch pointing to a failure

32
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:11.759
path. So the agent proceeds confidently. It has no mechanism to ask wait did this tool actually

33
00:03:11.759 --> 00:03:17.440
connect? Without explicit support for that question built into the tool response the model can't

34
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.399
distinguish ambiguous nothing from confirmed nothing unless the tool response is designed

35
00:03:22.399 --> 00:03:28.559
to make that distinction visible. That design choice is the entire fix. Access failures come

36
00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:34.639
in four main categories and each one produces an empty or incomplete response that looks like a

37
00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:40.160
successful query from the outside. Connection errors happen when the network or service endpoint

38
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.880
is completely unreachable. The tool never connects and returns nothing. Auth failures happen when

39
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.639
credentials are missing, expired, or revoked. The data store rejects the request before any search

40
00:03:52.639 --> 00:03:59.600
logic runs at all. Query timeouts happen when the data source is technically reachable but responds

41
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:06.000
too slowly. The tool gives up and returns empty and partial results are the trickiest. The data

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.880
source returns some records but silently truncates the rest due to an internal limit or error. All

43
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.480
four of these categories need to be caught at the wrapper layer and labeled explicitly before the

44
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.720
agent sees the response. Let's talk about the phrase the agent says when this goes wrong. I

45
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:31.200
couldn't find anything. That sentence implies a successful search with no matches. The user hears

46
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.760
that and concludes the information doesn't exist. They make decisions based on that conclusion. If

47
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.959
the real cause was an access failure, those decisions are built on a false premise. The

48
00:04:42.959 --> 00:04:49.200
correct response to an access failure is something like I was unable to check or the search tool

49
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.200
didn't complete successfully. Those phrases are truthful and they put the user on notice that the

50
00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.679
absence of information isn't a confirmed fact. The distinction isn't pedantic. It is the difference

51
00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:07.760
between a trustworthy agent and one that silently misleads users at exactly the moments they're

52
00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:14.399
relying on it most. The production cost of getting this wrong is high and it is delayed. When an

53
00:05:14.399 --> 00:05:20.160
agent returns a confident false negative, no exception is raised. The logs look clean. The

54
00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:26.160
tool ran. The agent responded. Everything appears to have worked correctly, but the user walked away

55
00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.600
with incorrect information and whatever decision they made based on that information is now in the

56
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:37.679
world. When you try to trace back a bad decision days or weeks later, you're looking for a silent

57
00:05:37.679 --> 00:05:44.079
failure in an agent session that left no error trace. That is extremely hard to debug and the

58
00:05:44.079 --> 00:05:50.000
compounding effect is worse. Users stop trusting the system after a few experiences like this,

59
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.239
even when they can't name exactly what went wrong. Silent false negatives erode trust faster than any

60
00:05:56.239 --> 00:06:04.079
visible error message. An empty tool result is an unanswered question, not an answer. The tool

61
00:06:04.079 --> 00:06:09.839
either confirmed nothing's there or never reached the source and those two carry opposite meanings.

62
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:16.959
That matters because your user hears I found nothing either way and acts on it. Just remember

63
00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:22.399
no exception fires here, so the bad decision is already in the world before anyone notices it.

64
00:06:23.119 --> 00:06:26.320
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

